## Status + Reason Architecture

`deriveDeviceHealth(entry)` in `@sentience/utils/selectors.ts` is the single shared status selector. Returns `{ status: DeviceStatus, reasons: StatusReason[] }` instead of a bare enum.

### Resolution rules (applied in order)

| Condition | Status | Reason(s) |
|-----------|--------|-----------|
| Raw status is "offline" | Offline | HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT |
| `lastSeen` > 120s ago | Offline | HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT |
| Battery ≤ 10% | Fault | BATTERY_CRITICAL |
| No battery on battery-powered device | Warning | BATTERY_MISSING |
| Battery 11–20% | Warning | LOW_BATTERY |
| Signal ≤ -110 dBm | Warning | WEAK_SIGNAL |
| Temperature ≥ 45°C | Warning | OVERHEAT |
| No issues found | (raw status, typically "online") | (empty) |

### Key rules
- **Fault** is only for critical battery or external diagnostic faults — NOT for 11-20% battery (that's Warning).
- **Externally powered devices** (controller/gateway/relay) are never penalized for missing battery data.
- `deriveDeviceStatus()` is kept as a legacy wrapper that calls `deriveDeviceHealth()` and extracts `.status`.

### UI display
- `StatusBadge` and `StatusDot` accept optional `reasons?: StatusReason[]` prop.
- When reasons are present, the component sets `title` attribute to "Warning — Low battery" etc.
- `formatStatusLabel(status, reasons)` → "Fault — Battery critical"
- `formatStatusReasons(reasons)` → "Low battery, Weak signal"

